  <h1>A tour of C++11 / 14 / 17 / 20 / 23</h1>
  <p class="why">Why this matters: your CMakeLists pin <code class="inline">CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17</code>. That number is doing real work &mdash; it decides which features you can use. Across the twelve lessons so far you've touched features from every standard from C++11 to C++20. This is the map that tells you which version gave you what, and what bumping the number would unlock.</p>
</header>

<h2>1. The concept &mdash; C++ has a release train</h2>

<p>Since 2011, ISO C++ releases on a three-year cadence: <b>C++11, C++14, C++17, C++20, C++23</b>, with C++26 in progress. Each release adds language features and library functions; older code keeps working. The number you set in your build system (<code class="inline">CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD</code>, <code class="inline">-std=c++17</code>) controls which features the compiler will accept.</p>

