<div class="callout">
  <h3>The mental model</h3>
  <p>C++11 is where "modern C++" begins; if you have that, you have 80% of what you write day-to-day. C++17 fills in usability gaps. C++20 is the next big language overhaul. C++23 is incremental but useful (especially <code class="inline">std::expected</code> and <code class="inline">std::print</code>).</p>
</div>

<h2>2. A minimal example &mdash; the same loop, four ways</h2>

<p>Read the same idea evolving across versions to feel how each one cleans the language up.</p>

<pre class="code"><span class="com">// C++03 (pre-modern): verbose, manual iterator types.</span>
<span class="kw">std</span>::vector&lt;<span class="kw">std</span>::string&gt; names;
names.push_back(<span class="str">"alice"</span>);
<span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">std</span>::vector&lt;<span class="kw">std</span>::string&gt;::iterator it = names.begin();
     it != names.end(); ++it) {
    <span class="kw">std</span>::cout &lt;&lt; *it &lt;&lt; <span class="str">"\n"</span>;
}

<span class="com">// C++11: auto + range-for + initializer list = legible.</span>
std::vector&lt;std::string&gt; names = {<span class="str">"alice"</span>, <span class="str">"bob"</span>};
<span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">const auto</span>&amp; n : names) std::cout &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; <span class="str">"\n"</span>;

<span class="com">// C++17: structured bindings make iterating maps clean.</span>
std::unordered_map&lt;std::string, <span class="typ">int</span>&gt; counts = {{<span class="str">"alice"</span>, <span class="num">1</span>}};
<span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">const auto</span>&amp; [name, count] : counts) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; <span class="str">": "</span> &lt;&lt; count &lt;&lt; <span class="str">"\n"</span>;
}

<span class="com">// C++23: std::print replaces &lt;iostream&gt; for many cases.</span>
std::print(<span class="str">"{}: {}\n"</span>, name, count);  <span class="com">// type-safe, no &lt;&lt;, no manual newlines</span></pre>

<p>Every C++03 line still compiles in C++23. The new versions don't remove anything (mostly); they give you cleaner ways to express the same intent.</p>

