<h3>What bumping to C++20 would give you</h3>

<p>If you change <code class="inline">CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17</code> to <code class="inline">20</code>, these become available:</p>

<table class="compare">
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it'd buy you</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><code class="inline">std::jthread</code></td><td>Auto-joining thread. Your <code class="inline">LlmClient</code> destructor wouldn't need the explicit <code class="inline">join()</code> &mdash; <code class="inline">jthread</code>'s destructor does it.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code class="inline">std::counting_semaphore</code> / <code class="inline">std::binary_semaphore</code></td><td>Lesson 0011's hypothetical GPU rate-limiter would actually be writable in standard C++.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code class="inline">std::span&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>Non-owning view over a contiguous range. Cleaner function signatures than <code class="inline">const std::vector&lt;T&gt;&amp;</code> when you don't care if the source is a vector or array.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Designated initializers</td><td><code class="inline">Persona{.name = "Wren", .role = "..."}</code> &mdash; brace-init that names the fields. Catches misordered arguments at compile time.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Three-way <code class="inline">operator&lt;=&gt;</code></td><td>Define one comparison operator and get all six. Useful when you add ordered NPC types.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Concepts</td><td>Constrain template parameters (<code class="inline">template &lt;Integer T&gt;</code>). Better error messages, expresses intent.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

