<h2>4. Often confused with...</h2>

<h3>Language standard vs library version</h3>
<p>"C++17" refers to <em>both</em> the language (new syntax: structured bindings, <code class="inline">if constexpr</code>) and the library (<code class="inline">std::optional</code>, <code class="inline">std::filesystem</code>). They were standardised together but they're implemented separately: the compiler does the language parts; the standard library implementation (<code class="inline">libstdc++</code>, <code class="inline">libc++</code>, MSVC STL) does the library parts. Some compiler versions support the language but not the full library yet, or vice versa.</p>

<h3><code class="inline">-std=c++17</code> vs compiler version</h3>
<p><code class="inline">-std=c++17</code> sets the dialect &mdash; what the compiler accepts. The compiler version sets <em>how completely</em> that dialect is implemented. GCC 6 will accept <code class="inline">-std=c++17</code> but doesn't fully implement it; GCC 11 does. When something "doesn't compile", the question is which: dialect not enabled, or version too old to have the feature.</p>

<h3>"Modern C++"</h3>
<p>The phrase is fuzzy. The Bjarne-coined meaning is "C++ written in the C++11 idiom or later": auto, smart pointers, range-for, no manual <code class="inline">new</code>/<code class="inline">delete</code>. Doesn't necessarily mean C++23. Your code is modern C++ in the original sense even though it's pinned to C++17.</p>

<h3>Forward and backward compatibility</h3>
<p><b>Backward compatibility</b>: C++17 code compiles in C++20 mode (almost always). <b>Forward compatibility</b>: C++17 code does <em>not</em> use C++20 features. The standards committee tries hard not to break old code; the corollary is that "old features that aged poorly" stay in the language (e.g. <code class="inline">printf</code>, raw <code class="inline">new</code>).</p>

<h3>Compiler-specific extensions vs standard features</h3>
<p>GCC and Clang offer extensions that aren't standard C++ &mdash; nested functions, variable-length arrays, designated initializers (which became standard in C++20). If you write code that uses an extension without realising, you may be locked to that compiler. The <code class="inline">-pedantic</code> flag tells GCC/Clang to warn about extensions; <code class="inline">CMAKE_CXX_EXTENSIONS OFF</code> in your CMakeLists.txt does the equivalent &mdash; you have this set, which is why your code is portable.</p>

<h2>5. Quiz</h2>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Your CMakeLists.txt has <code class="inline">set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)</code>. You write <code class="inline">std::span&lt;int&gt; v</code> in a new file. What happens?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="span isn't in C++17 — it was added in C++20. The compiler will refuse it under -std=c++17 (or whatever your CMake sets).">
      Compiles fine &mdash; <code class="inline">std::span</code> is a C++17 feature.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. std::span is C++20. Under CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17, the compiler doesn't expose it, and the include of <span> will fail (or std::span will be undeclared). Bumping to CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20 enables it.">
      Compile error &mdash; <code class="inline">std::span</code> is C++20; you need to bump <code class="inline">CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD</code> to 20.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Compilers don't 'auto-detect' that you want a newer standard. The setting in CMakeLists is what controls the dialect; you'd have to change it.">
      Compiles and works &mdash; the compiler automatically detects you need C++20.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Even old GCC versions don't 'crash on undeclared identifier' — they error at compile time with a message. The behaviour at runtime is moot because there's no executable.">
      Compiles, but crashes at runtime on the first access.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Which of these is the <em>oldest</em> standard that gave you the syntax <code class="inline">for (const auto&amp; x : container)</code>?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="C++03 had no range-for. Iterating took explicit iterators or indices. Range-for arrived with the C++11 'modern C++' wave.">
      C++03 &mdash; range-for has been there since the beginning.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Range-for is one of the headline C++11 quality-of-life features, alongside auto, lambdas, and move semantics. It works on anything with begin()/end() — vectors, arrays, deques, maps.">
      C++11 &mdash; range-for was a headline 'modern C++' feature.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Range-for predates C++14; it's in C++11. C++14 added small refinements but range-for was already there.">
      C++14 &mdash; introduced as part of C++14's quality-of-life pass.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Way too late. Range-for has been ubiquitous since 2011.">
      C++17 &mdash; came with structured bindings.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">The lesson 0007 refactor uses <code class="inline">std::make_unique&lt;OllamaBackend&gt;(host, port, model)</code>. The user's CMake is on C++17. Which earlier standard introduced this function (and when would you not be able to use it)?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="make_unique is famously a C++14 addition — it was the oversight that C++11 'forgot' to ship with make_shared. C++11 has make_shared but not make_unique.">
      C++11 &mdash; alongside <code class="inline">std::unique_ptr</code> itself.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. unique_ptr was C++11 but make_unique didn't ship until C++14 — a famous oversight. If a codebase is pinned to C++11, you have to write std::unique_ptr<T>(new T(args)) by hand. CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 11 is the one place this lesson's feature wouldn't compile.">
      C++14 &mdash; <code class="inline">make_unique</code> was famously omitted from C++11 and added later.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="It predates C++17 by a release. C++17 is when std::optional and std::filesystem shipped; make_unique has been around for 11 years.">
      C++17 &mdash; came with the smart-pointer overhaul.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="Adding it in C++20 would have been very late given how widely used unique_ptr was. It's C++14.">
      C++20 &mdash; alongside <code class="inline">std::span</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">Your <code class="inline">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs/src/main.cpp</code> uses <code class="inline">fs::path</code>, <code class="inline">fs::exists</code>, <code class="inline">fs::current_path</code>. Which standard first made these portable across compilers?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="C++03 had nothing like std::filesystem. Boost.Filesystem was the de-facto choice for years before standardisation.">
      C++03 &mdash; the standard library has always shipped a filesystem API.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="C++11 added threads and atomics but not filesystem. The TS for filesystem came later.">
      C++11 &mdash; alongside <code class="inline">std::thread</code>.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. std::filesystem was a Technical Specification that merged into the C++17 standard library. It's a canonical reason most codebases jumped from C++14 to C++17 — finally, portable file paths in the standard library. Your CMake target is on 17, which is why fs::path works without extra dependencies.">
      C++17 &mdash; <code class="inline">std::filesystem</code> finalised the long-running TS.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="C++20 added concepts and ranges; filesystem predates it by a release.">
      C++20 &mdash; along with <code class="inline">std::span</code> and concepts.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

<div class="question" data-question>
  <p class="q">A teammate says "let's add <code class="inline">std::expected</code> to <code class="inline">ChatReply</code>; it'd clean up the <code class="inline">ok</code>/<code class="inline">errorMessage</code> pair." You agree the design is nicer. What's the <em>one</em> change to make this concretely possible in your code?</p>
  <ul class="options">
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="A header-only library can add an analog (tl::expected), but the standard one specifically requires C++23. The 'one change' the question asks about, sticking to the standard library, is the CMake bump.">
      Vendor a header-only <code class="inline">tl::expected</code> &mdash; no compiler change needed.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. std::expected is C++23. Your CMake is pinned to 17. Bumping CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD to 23 (and confirming your toolchain supports it — GCC 14+, Clang 17+, MSVC 2026+) unlocks it. The rest of your code keeps working.">
      Bump <code class="inline">CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD</code> from 17 to 23 (and check the toolchain supports it).
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="std::expected has been in the standard since C++23 (Oct 2024). No 'experimental' header needed in modern compilers.">
      Switch the include to <code class="inline">&lt;experimental/expected&gt;</code>, since the type isn't yet standardised.
    </li>
    <li data-correct="false" data-why="There's no global compiler flag that enables 'all standard library extensions.' The dialect (-std=) is the mechanism; bumping it is the change.">
      Pass <code class="inline">-fstdlib-extensions</code> to the compiler to opt in.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
</div>

